00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:32.810
<v Bruce Roderick>That was for a shooting and-- so on drugs. Summer of '69, I believe-- '69, yeah. I was going to go to Woodstock, I got arrested that weekend. I received three year sentence. I made parole, I was out for a month, I went back then. And then I started robbing drug dealers.
<v Bruce Roderick>是因为一场枪击案和……关于毒品。大概是69年的夏天吧——69年，没错。我本来要去参加伍德斯托克音乐节，结果那个周末我被逮捕了。我被判了三年。我获得了假释，出来一个月，然后又回去了。然后我开始抢劫毒贩。

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:38.720
<v Emily Hsiao>Wow. What was robbing drug dealers like? How'd you do that?
<v Emily Hsiao>哇。抢劫毒贩是什么样的？你怎么做到的？

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:45.600
<v Bruce Roderick>I would knock on the door, they would open the door, I'd put a gun in their face, go into the house, and take the drugs.
<v Bruce Roderick>我会敲门，他们会开门，我用枪指着他们的脸，进入屋里，拿走毒品。

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:47.500
<v Emily Hsiao>Have you ever killed anyone?
<v Emily Hsiao>你杀过人吗？

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:55.020
<v Bruce Roderick>No. Why would I sit here and admit to something like that anyway, if I did?
<v Bruce Roderick>没有。再说，如果我杀了人，我干嘛还要坐在这儿承认那种事呢？

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:56.400
<v Emily Hsiao>Yeah, good question.
<v Emily Hsiao>没错，好问题。

00:20:56.400 --> 00:21:08.100
<v Bruce Roderick>Right?
<v Bruce Roderick>对吧？

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:28.660
<v Bruce Roderick>I was robbing drug dealers, and that was my career right up until 1985, when I went to a house. I went into a house in Centerville, and it was-- police were in the house. They posed as drug dealers.
<v Bruce Roderick>我一直在抢劫毒贩，那是我的职业，直到1985年，我去了一所房子。我走进森特维尔的一所房子，里面……警察就在屋里。他们伪装成毒贩。

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:48.580
<v Bruce Roderick>And there was a confrontation in the house I ended up going to prison for. I spent 12 years in jail on that one. Went back in again for selling heroin. Got out, went back in again for selling heroin. Now I just got out again. Now here I am, I been out two years. Almost three years.
<v Bruce Roderick>屋里发生了对峙，我最后因此进了监狱。我在那次案件中坐了12年牢。又因为卖海洛因进去。出来后，又因为卖海洛因进去。现在我刚出来。这就是我了，我已经出来两年了。快三年了。

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:55.170
<v Bruce Roderick>This is the longest that I've been out here free and haven't been using.
<v Bruce Roderick>这是我出来自由生活且没用药最长的一次。

00:21:55.170 --> 00:21:57.640
<v Emily Hsiao>What's prison like?
<v Emily Hsiao>监狱是什么样的？

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:33.790
<v Bruce Roderick>What's it like? Prison is very-- there's racial alliances. You have to hang with your own kind. And if you let a black or a Spanish or somebody else not of your race to take advantage of you sexually or whatever, then you become a punk, I guess-- you want some gum?
<v Bruce Roderick>是什么样的？监狱里非常……有种族联盟。你必须和自己种族的人待在一起。如果你让黑人或西班牙裔或者其他非你种族的人在性等方面占你便宜，那你就会变成一个“小混混”，我猜——你想吃口香糖吗？

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:45.220
<v Bruce Roderick>Which means anybody can take whatever they want from you, because you didn't stick up for yourself. You're fair game. I think I even got some pictures here.
<v Bruce Roderick>这意味着任何人都可以从你身上拿走任何东西，因为你没为自己挺身而出。你是被任意宰割的猎物。我想我这儿甚至有几张照片。

00:22:45.220 --> 00:22:46.650
<v Emily Hsiao>Pictures from prison?
<v Emily Hsiao>监狱的照片？

00:22:46.650 --> 00:23:02.540
<v Bruce Roderick>Yeah, I got a couple, I think. That's me when I was younger. I was a jailbird. That's my niece, my niece. That's me in a bar in Boston. That's a jail picture.
<v Bruce Roderick>是啊，我想我有几张。那是年轻时的我。我当时是个囚犯。这是我侄女。那是波士顿一家酒吧里的我。那是一张监狱照。

00:23:02.540 --> 00:23:20.460
<v Bruce Roderick>That was my father. That's me in my mother's house. My parents were devastated. No one in my family's ever gotten in trouble before. But they always stuck by me.
<v Bruce Roderick>那是我的父亲。那是母亲家里的我。我的父母当时非常崩溃。我们家没人惹过麻烦。但他们一直支持我。

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:24.240
<v Emily Hsiao>Do you think your parents should have practiced tougher love?
<v Emily Hsiao>你觉得你父母应该对你进行更严厉的管教吗？

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:37.760
<v Bruce Roderick>What, beat me more? What's tough love? They didn't say, yeah, yeah, here's $20, go get high-- no. My father was disgusted by my behavior. My mother was heartbroken. I my mother's favorite.
<v Bruce Roderick>什么，多打我几下？什么叫严厉的管教？他们没说过，好的好的，给你20美元，去爽一把——不。我父亲对我的行为感到厌恶。我母亲心碎了。我是母亲最疼爱的。

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:55.210
<v Bruce Roderick>Of course not, they didn't want me to do the things that I did. Well, you know, what are you going to do? You don't think of nothing else but yourself when you're doing those things you do.
<v Bruce Roderick>当然不是，他们不想让我做我做的那些事。嗯，你知道，你能怎么样呢？当你做那些事的时候，你脑子里想的只有你自己。

00:23:55.210 --> 00:24:13.060
<v Bruce Roderick>This is North Fork Prison here. That's me there. That's one of my good friends. He's a dope fiend. This guy hung around for like 10 years, right? And he was like my best friend, and I didn't know he was in for rape. And that's prison.
<v Bruce Roderick>这是北福克监狱。那是那时的我。那是我一个好朋友。他是个毒瘾君子。这家伙待了大概10年吧？他曾经是我最好的朋友，但我不知道他是因为强奸入狱的。这就是监狱。

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:17.470
<v Emily Hsiao>Whoa. I haven't even gotten to your tattoo yet. I've just been so enthralled.
<v Emily Hsiao>哇。我还没谈到你的纹身呢。我只是太着迷了。

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:31.380
<v Bruce Roderick>There's the tattoo. See it? It's not even that big, you can barely even see it. And I got it done around 1986, '87. It was supposed to have white power.
<v Bruce Roderick>这就是纹身。看到了吗？它甚至没那么大，你几乎都看不见。我大概在86年、87年纹的。本来是为了展示“白人力量”。

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:50.480
<v Bruce Roderick>I was covering up a question mark I had right there when I was a kid. And this guy, Willie Durette, he did a lot of my tattoos. I think he was in my cell doing a tattoo for somebody else. And I had some biker book or something. When I saw that tattoo, I asked Willie if he could cover the question mark up a bit.
<v Bruce Roderick>当时我想遮盖小时候纹的一个问号。那个叫威利·杜雷特的家伙帮我做了很多纹身。我想当时他在我的牢房里帮别人纹身。我手里有一本机车杂志之类的。看到那个纹身时，我问威利能不能帮我遮盖一下那个问号。

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:57.080
<v Bruce Roderick>And I put that on, and now I want this covered up. That's all I want, is that blacked out.
<v Bruce Roderick>然后我就纹了那个，现在我想把它遮盖掉。我只想要那个，把它涂黑。

00:24:57.080 --> 00:24:59.970
<v Emily Hsiao>Did your buddies get swastikas as well?
<v Emily Hsiao>你那些哥们儿也纹了纳粹党徽吗？

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:17.400
<v Bruce Roderick>Oh yeah, most of the white guys back then had swastikas. Swastikas are SWP-- Supreme White Power. FTW-- a lot of them had that. That used to be another big one. Fuck the world.
<v Bruce Roderick>哦，是的，当时大多数白人家伙都有纳粹党徽。纳粹党徽意味着SWP——至高无上的白人力量。FTW——很多人纹了那个。那曾经是另一个大热门。去他妈的世界（Fuck the world）。

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:22.480
<v Emily Hsiao>Did the swastika represent Nazi ideas in prison?
<v Emily Hsiao>在监狱里，纳粹党徽代表纳粹思想吗？

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:29.990
<v Bruce Roderick>No, it doesn't represent nothing. It's just a fucking tattoo. I don't have anything against Jewish people.
<v Bruce Roderick>不，它什么也不代表。那只是个该死的纹身。我对犹太人没什么成见。

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:35.720
<v Emily Hsiao>But that symbol, the swastika, says otherwise, right?
<v Emily Hsiao>但纳粹党徽那个符号说明的情况相反吧？

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:39.170
<v Bruce Roderick>The swastika was just a symbol, period.
<v Bruce Roderick>纳粹党徽只是个符号，仅此而已。

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:42.030
<v Emily Hsiao>What does it mean to you?
<v Emily Hsiao>它对你意味着什么？

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:55.100
<v Bruce Roderick>I like the way it looks. I used to have a big flag. Above my wall, I had a big swastika flag. I gave it to my roommate up in Revere before I moved out.
<v Bruce Roderick>我喜欢它看起来的样子。我曾经有一面大旗。墙上方，我挂着一面纳粹党徽大旗。我搬走之前把它送给了我在里维尔的室友。

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:58.730
<v Emily Hsiao>But you know that it carries so many connotations?
<v Emily Hsiao>但你知道它承载着许多内涵吧？

00:25:58.730 --> 00:26:14.260
<v Bruce Roderick>That's exactly why I'm taking it off my arm. I read an obituary about this Jewish guy that was in the camps. And this guy worked in the gas chambers, the showers.
<v Bruce Roderick>那正是我想把它从胳膊上弄掉的原因。我读了一篇关于那个在集中营里的犹太人的讣告。那个家伙在毒气室、淋浴房工作。

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:25.090
<v Bruce Roderick>They would take the bodies out of the shower and bring them to be burned. This guy, that was his job. That's why he was able to survive. He was in there towards the end of the war.
<v Bruce Roderick>他们会把尸体从淋浴房拖出来带去焚烧。那个家伙，那就是他的工作。这就是他能活下来的原因。他在战争快结束时待在里面。

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:42.440
<v Bruce Roderick>But anyway, he just died recently. And in his obituary, it said he had written this book. And I read the book. And then, I just didn't want the tattoo anymore. Because it's a very offensive thing.
<v Bruce Roderick>但总之，他最近刚去世。在他的讣告里，说他写了这本书。我读了那本书。然后，我就不想再要这个纹身了。因为它是一件非常冒犯的东西。

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:54.200
<v Bruce Roderick>And I am getting older. I don't want to die and have God see that on my arm.
<v Bruce Roderick>而且我年纪大了。我不想死的时候上帝在我的胳膊上看到那个东西。

00:26:54.200 --> 00:27:11.930
<v Bruce Roderick>I spent over 30 years in prison. I'd like to have those years back. I never had any kids. My mother and father are both dead. They never saw me succeed in nothing. I'm 61, I don't have shit for money.
<v Bruce Roderick>我在监狱里度过了30多年。我真想把那些岁月找回来。我从来没生过孩子。我的母亲和父亲都去世了。他们从未见我成功过任何事情。我已经61岁了，钱一点也没有。

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:25.670
<v Bruce Roderick>All of a sudden, I woke up and I was 60 years old. It just flew by. The time flew right by, all behind drugs. And that's it. Are we done?
<v Bruce Roderick>突然之间，我醒来发现自己已经60岁了。时间飞逝。时间就在毒品中飞逝而去。就是这样。我们要结束了吗？

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:26.500
<v Emily Hsiao>No.
<v Emily Hsiao>没有。

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:27.120
<v Bruce Roderick>Yeah, we are.
<v Bruce Roderick>是的，我们要结束了。

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:28.080
<v Emily Hsiao>We are?
<v Emily Hsiao>我们结束了吗？

00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:28.860
<v Bruce Roderick>Basically, yeah.
<v Bruce Roderick>基本上是这样。

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:29.430
<v Emily Hsiao>I'm sorry.
<v Emily Hsiao>我很抱歉。

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:38.970
<v Bruce Roderick>But where are you going? Where do you have to go? I'll give you a ride, I'm saying. This interview is over. I'm sorry.
<v Bruce Roderick>但你要去哪儿？你必须去哪儿？我是说，我可以载你一程。这个采访结束了。我很抱歉。

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:39.140
<v Emily Hsiao>That's OK.
<v Emily Hsiao>没关系。

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:40.120
<v Bruce Roderick>Did it go all right?
<v Bruce Roderick>还顺利吗？

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:42.590
<v Emily Hsiao>Yeah.
<v Emily Hsiao>是的。

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:51.730
<v Bruce Roderick>We gotta defrost. All right. What did you think, I'm a dirty racist?
<v Bruce Roderick>我们得解冻了。好了。你以为我是个肮脏的种族主义者？

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:53.870
<v Emily Hsiao>I didn't know.
<v Emily Hsiao>我不知道。

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:59.670
<v Bruce Roderick>What do you think now? Still that dirty racist? You still got that fucking thing on?
<v Bruce Roderick>你现在怎么想？还是那个肮脏的种族主义者？你身上还有那该死的东西？

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:09.930
<v Emily Hsiao>Yeah. I do. Right on.
<v Emily Hsiao>是的。我有。没错。

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:11.520
<v Bruce Roderick>All right. You gotta get out of here.
<v Bruce Roderick>好吧。你得离开这儿。

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:13.550
<v Emily Hsiao>OK, thanks.
<v Emily Hsiao>好的，谢谢。

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:15.580
<v Bruce Roderick>My patience is wearing thin.
<v Bruce Roderick>我的耐心快磨没了。

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:16.310
<v Emily Hsiao>Thanks, Bruce.
<v Emily Hsiao>谢谢你，布鲁斯。

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:16.990
<v Bruce Roderick>Have fun.
<v Bruce Roderick>玩得开心。

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:18.190
<v Emily Hsiao>Take care.
<v Emily Hsiao>保重。

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:20.410
<v Bruce Roderick>You better say I'm a nice fucking guy in this.
<v Bruce Roderick>你最好在节目里说我是个他妈的好人。

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:24.220
<v Emily Hsiao>All righty. Thank you so much.
<v Emily Hsiao>没问题。非常感谢。

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:46.490
<v Bruce Roderick>What's up, [INAUDIBLE]. Why the fuck didn't you go pick up Charlie that day? He is so fucking pissed.
<v Bruce Roderick>怎么了，[听不清]。你他妈那天为什么没去接查理？他他妈气疯了。

00:28:46.490 --> 00:29:03.900
<v Ira Glass>That story by Emily Hsiao-- it's one of the very first radio stories she's ever made. She created it at something called the Transom Story Workshop, which takes people who have never done radio-- in eight weeks, it trains them to make stories in Woods Hole, Massachusetts. You can Google Transom Story Workshop, if you're curious about that.
<v Ira Glass>艾米丽·萧做的那个故事——这是她制作的第一批广播故事之一。她在Transom故事研讨会创造了这个故事，该研讨会招收从未做过广播的人——在马萨诸塞州伍兹霍尔，用八周时间培训他们制作故事。如果你对此感到好奇，可以去谷歌搜索Transom Story Workshop。

00:29:03.900 --> 00:29:14.230
<v Ira Glass>The music was created for the story by Merel van Dijk and Anthony Barilla from the band Merel & Tony. You can find other music of theirs on SoundCloud-- Merel and Tony.
<v Ira Glass>音乐由梅瑞尔与托尼乐队的梅瑞尔·范·迪克和安东尼·巴里拉为故事创作。你可以在SoundCloud上找到他们的其他音乐——Merel and Tony。

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:20.230
<v Ira Glass>We first aired this story back in 2014. Since then, Bruce found somebody to black out his tattoo.
<v Ira Glass>我们第一次播出这个故事是在2014年。从那时起，布鲁斯找到了遮盖纹身的人。

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:49.550
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "REGRET" BY ST. VINCENT]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 圣文森特的《Regret》]

00:29:49.550 --> 00:30:01.140
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a story with original songs by Stephin Merritt. I'll explain what that's about after the break. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，是一个由斯蒂芬·梅里特创作原创歌曲的故事。休息之后我会解释那是什么情况。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播，我们的节目继续。

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:10.460
<v Ira Glass>This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. On today's show, our theme, of course--
<v Ira Glass>《这是美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。当然，每周在我们的节目中，我们会选择一个主题，带给你关于该主题的不同故事。在今天的节目中，我们的主题，当然——

00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:14.420
<v The Texas Tenors>(SINGING) Regrets, I've had a few.
<v The Texas Tenors>（歌唱）悔恨，我有过一些。

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:22.670
<v Ira Glass>That's right. Stories of regret. We've arrived at Act Two of our show. Act Two-- This is Just as Hard for Me as It is for You.
<v Ira Glass>没错。悔恨的故事。我们来到了节目的第二幕。第二幕——这对我来说和你一样难。

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:35.010
<v Ira Glass>One of the most basic parts of being a parent is trying to sort through a bunch of options and figure out what is going to be best for your kids. Everything from what foods they should eat to where you're going to live so they'll have decent schools.
<v Ira Glass>为人父母最基本的部分之一就是试着梳理一大堆选择，并弄清楚什么对孩子最好。从他们应该吃什么食物，到你打算住哪儿好让他们有像样的学校。

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:48.750
<v Ira Glass>This next story is about a father trying to do that, trying to figure out what's best for his children, and having some regrets about how things worked out. His name is Will Ream. And the choices Will has had to make have been pretty dramatic ones.
<v Ira Glass>下一个故事关于一个试图这样做的父亲，试着弄清楚什么对孩子最好，并且对事情的结局感到一些悔恨。他的名字叫威尔·里姆。而威尔不得不做出的选择是非常戏剧性的。

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:58.980
<v Ira Glass>And to tell a story, we're going to try something that we have never attempted before. We asked a songwriter, a great songwriter-- Stephin Merritt, from the band The Magnetic Fields, and other bands also-- to collaborate with us.
<v Ira Glass>为了讲述这个故事，我们将尝试以前从未尝试过的事情。我们邀请了一位伟大的词曲作者——来自Magnetic Fields乐队和其他乐队的斯蒂芬·梅里特——与我们合作。

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:17.030
<v Ira Glass>And mostly, as you'll hear, Stephin has composed music that's going to run underneath the narration and the quotes, like we always have in our stories. Like the music that we had written for Act One of our show today. Though in a couple places-- and really, it's just a few places-- Stephin wrote songs. Like, real songs, with words, that he'll sing.
<v Ira Glass>而且正如你将听到的，斯蒂芬创作的音乐主要会在叙述和语录下方流淌，就像我们在故事里一直做的那样。就像我们在今天节目第一幕中创作的音乐。不过在几个地方——实际上，只是几个地方——斯蒂芬写了歌。就像，真正的歌曲，有歌词，由他演唱。

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:22.690
<v Stephin  Merritt>(SINGING) I'll sing something that sounds like this.
<v Stephin  Merritt>（歌唱）我会唱些听起来像这样的歌。

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:36.770
<v Ira Glass>So during the story that you're about to hear, when Stephin is singing, he's going to be singing things that the dad in the story, Will Ream, actually said. The lyrics are all verbatim quotes. Or they're as close to it as Stephin could get within the structure of a song.
<v Ira Glass>所以，在接下来你将听到的故事中，当斯蒂芬唱歌时，他唱的内容正是故事里的父亲威尔·里姆所说的话。歌词全是原文引用。或者是在歌曲结构下斯蒂芬能做到的尽可能接近的引用。

00:31:36.770 --> 00:31:48.060
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Miki Meek, first encountered Will Ream when Will Ream had just left everything he had ever known. He had grown up in this incredibly isolated town called Colorado City, in the deserts of northern Arizona.
<v Ira Glass>我们的一位制片人，米基·米克，第一次遇到威尔·里姆时，威尔·里姆刚离开他所知的一切。他在这个难以置信的孤立小镇科罗拉多城长大，位于亚利桑那州北部的沙漠中。

00:31:48.060 --> 00:32:04.540
<v Ira Glass>He'd been raised inside this religious group there-- a fundamentalist group, a whole community of people that had split off from the mainstream Mormon church over a century ago. Has lived out there in Colorado City in the desert since. So here is the story. Here's Miki.
<v Ira Glass>他在那里被一个宗教团体抚养长大——一个原教旨主义团体，整个社区的人在一百多年前就脱离了主流摩门教。一直生活在沙漠里的科罗拉多城。这就是故事。这是米基。

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:13.680
<v Miki Meek>So when I first met Will in 2012, he was living in a rundown house outside of Salt Lake City, sleeping on a mattress on the floor, and just trying to figure out how to start his life over.
<v Miki Meek>所以，当我2012年第一次见到威尔时，他住在盐湖城外一栋破旧的房子里，睡在地板上的床垫上，正试图弄清楚如何重新开始他的生活。

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:29.190
<v Miki Meek>He wasn't even sure how he'd gotten to this point, because three years earlier, he was a married man living in Colorado City. He and his wife had four girls and a little boy, and they were surrounded by family and people he'd gone to church with his whole life. He was happy.
<v Miki Meek>他甚至不确定自己是怎么走到这一步的，因为三年前，他还是个住在科罗拉多城的已婚男人。他和妻子有四个女儿和一个小男孩，周围都是家人和他一生一起去教堂的人。他当时很幸福。

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:42.780
<v Miki Meek>But then things started getting strange at church. Church leaders went into this weird paranoid mode, seeing outside threats everywhere, and in everything. And they began to ask Will and some other men to do all sorts of stuff they hadn't asked for before.
<v Miki Meek>但后来教堂里的事情开始变得奇怪起来。教堂领袖进入了一种奇怪的偏执状态，看到处都是外部威胁，在任何事情中都看到威胁。他们开始要求威尔和其他一些男人做各种他们以前从未要求过的事情。

00:32:42.780 --> 00:32:56.930
<v Miki Meek>The church leadership had people it wanted to intimidate. These were former church members, and people who were questioning them. So they'd send Will and these other guys from church out in the middle of the night to vandalize these people's stuff-- disable farm equipment, change locks on gates.
<v Miki Meek>教堂领导层有一些想要恐吓的人。这些人是前教堂成员，以及质疑他们的人。所以他们会派威尔和教堂里的这些人深夜出去破坏这些人的东西——拆除农具，更换门锁。

00:32:56.930 --> 00:33:01.120
<v Miki Meek>And the church told Will and the other men-- you can't tell your wives.
<v Miki Meek>教堂告诉威尔和其他男人——你们不能告诉你们的妻子。

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:15.350
<v Will Ream>My gut reaction was if I am married to her, then we are supposed to be as one. So why am I supposed to keep secrets from her? Because it seems to me like that would be detrimental to our relationship.
<v Will Ream>我的本能反应是如果我跟她结婚了，那么我们应该成为一体。所以我为什么要对她保守秘密呢？因为在我看来这会对我们的关系有害。

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:17.780
<v Miki Meek>Did you ask that? Did you feel like you could ask that?
<v Miki Meek>你问过吗？你觉得你能问吗？

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:19.790
<v Will Ream>I couldn't ask that.
<v Will Ream>我不能问。

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:21.670
<v Miki Meek>That's just not something that was done?
<v Miki Meek>那根本就不是能做的事情？

00:33:21.670 --> 00:33:24.380
<v Will Ream>No. That wasn't something we did.
<v Will Ream>不。那不是我们做的事情。

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:34.490
<v Miki Meek>Will says, he felt like he and his wife had a good marriage, that they were close. And now here he was, leaving in the middle of the night and staying out for hours, and not telling her why.
<v Miki Meek>威尔说，他觉得自己和妻子婚姻美满，他们很亲近。而现在，他不得不深夜离家，待上好几个小时，却不告诉她原因。

00:33:34.490 --> 00:33:47.940
<v Miki Meek>Church leaders told Will, you don't owe her an explanation. If she wants answers, she should pray harder. Even for a young religious wife who was used to a man being the final word, this was too much. It dragged on for almost a year.
<v Miki Meek>教堂领袖告诉威尔，你不需要向她解释。如果她想要答案，她应该更努力地祈祷。即使对于一个习惯于男人拥有最终决定权的年轻宗教妻子来说，这也太过分了。这持续了将近一年。

00:33:47.940 --> 00:34:11.970
<v Will Ream>She would say, like, what were you guys doing last night? And I would say, I can't tell you. Or, a man doesn't need to tell his wife everything. And I would use that line that they told me, which, now-- even just now, when I said it, it makes me go tense. That's something that hurts, that I was that stupid.
<v Will Ream>她会说，你们昨晚在做什么？我会说，我不能告诉你。或者，男人不需要告诉妻子每件事。我会用他们教我的那句话，现在——甚至刚才，当我说了那句话时，它都让我感到紧张。那是一件伤害人的事，我当时竟然那么蠢。

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:33.330
<v Will Ream>And there were quite a few nights where I remember she would be really distraught. And she would go into the bathroom, into the shower, and just weep it out. And I would turn my back on her. And force myself to not feel.
<v Will Ream>有几个晚上我记得她非常痛苦。她会走进浴室，进入淋浴间，只是哭出来。而我背对着她。强迫自己不去感受。

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:56.980
<v Will Ream>If there was anything I could ever do to take that back, I would have. But I drove her to a place where she was very depressed, and very hurt, because she loved me with all her heart.
<v Will Ream>如果我能做任何事来挽回那一切，我会的。但我把她带到了一个她非常抑郁，非常受伤的地方，因为她全心全意地爱我。

00:34:56.980 --> 00:35:07.860
<v Will Ream>If I'd known at that point what I was choosing at that point, if I was choosing her or the church, I would have chosen her. But I didn't even realize it was really that big of a problem at that point.
<v Will Ream>如果我在那时候知道我正在做出什么选择，如果是在她和教会之间做选择，我会选择她的。但我当时甚至没意识到那是个大问题。

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:19.300
<v Miki Meek>One day Will came home from work and his wife was gone. She left him a note saying she needed to think. He tried to work it out with her for a year and a half after that. She even moved back home for a while.
<v Miki Meek>有一天威尔下班回家，发现妻子不见了。她给他留了一张纸条，说她需要思考。在那之后，他试着挽回了一年半。她甚至搬回来住了一阵子。

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:30.310
<v Miki Meek>But in the end, Will says she wanted nothing to do with her old life. She didn't want to be a mom or a wife, and she checked out on Will and their five kids. She wouldn't talk to me for this story.
<v Miki Meek>但最终，威尔说她不想再跟以前的生活有任何牵连。她不想当妈妈或妻子，她丢下了威尔和他们的五个孩子。她不愿意和我谈这个故事。

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:42.710
<v Miki Meek>But Will says they had gotten married when she was 15. So when she left him, she was still in her early 20s. She had never experienced anything outside the church. Now she started seeing other men, and drinking.
<v Miki Meek>但威尔说他们结婚时她才15岁。所以当她离开他时，她才20出头。她从未体验过教会之外的任何东西。现在她开始见其他男人，喝酒。

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:50.340
<v Miki Meek>Will was devastated, and he resented the church for causing their breakup. And things were getting even weirder at church.
<v Miki Meek>威尔崩溃了，他憎恨教会导致了他们的分手。而且教堂里的情况变得更诡异了。

00:35:50.340 --> 00:36:03.950
<v Miki Meek>Will's particular branch of fundamentalist Mormonism is headed by the Prophet Warren Jeffs. You may have heard of him. He got investigated in Utah, Arizona, and Texas for sexual assault and marrying underage girls. He was sentenced to life in prison.
<v Miki Meek>威尔所在的那个原教旨主义摩门教分支由先知沃伦·杰夫斯领导。你可能听说过他。他在犹他州、亚利桑那州和得克萨斯州因性侵和与未成年女孩结婚受到调查。他被判处终身监禁。

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:17.830
<v Miki Meek>Will and the other followers were told if they had enough faith, God would release Jeffs from prison. And so to test their faith, lots of strange new rules came down. Don't eat sugar. Only wear homemade clothing-- and it can't be the color red.
<v Miki Meek>威尔和其他追随者被告知，只要他们有足够的信心，上帝就会把杰夫斯从监狱里释放出来。因此为了考验他们的信心，发布了许多奇怪的新规则。不要吃糖。只能穿自制的衣服——而且不能是红色的。

00:36:17.830 --> 00:36:23.150
<v Miki Meek>Will remembers one Sunday, a new set of rules came down, specifically aimed at everyone's kids.
<v Miki Meek>威尔记得一个周日，发布了一套新规则，专门针对所有人的孩子。

00:36:23.150 --> 00:36:39.290
<v Will Ream>They talked about how the kids weren't supposed to play. I think that was probably one of the biggest things for me, was they weren't supposed to play just to have fun anymore. They were asking the people to get rid of their children's toys, and to not allow them to ride their bicycles.
<v Will Ream>他们谈到孩子们不应该玩耍。我想那大概是对我来说最大的事情之一，他们不再被允许为了开心而玩耍了。他们要求人们处理掉孩子的玩具，并且不准他们骑自行车。

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:57.450
<v Will Ream>They basically were saying that we needed to sacrifice everything that meant anything to us. Everything from our desires to our physical belongings to our homes to our families to everything. We needed to be willing to sacrifice everything for the good of the prophet.
<v Will Ream>他们基本上是在说，我们需要牺牲掉对我们意味着一切的东西。从我们的欲望到我们的实物财产，到我们的家，到我们的家人，到一切。为了先知的好处，我们必须愿意牺牲一切。

00:36:57.450 --> 00:37:07.100
<v Will Ream>I just remember leaving that place feeling like shit, thinking, is this really what I want? Is this really what I want for my kids?
<v Will Ream>我只记得离开那里时感觉糟糕透了，心想，这真的是我想要的吗？这真的是我为我的孩子们想要的吗？

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:12.020
<v Miki Meek>Will got desperate. He reached out to his bishop at church.
<v Miki Meek>威尔绝望了。他联系了教会的主教。

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:20.930
<v Will Ream>I told him, I says, I need some help here. I'm losing it. And he just says, you need to pray more. You need to get over it. That's all he said-- you need to pray more.
<v Will Ream>我告诉他，我说，我需要帮助。我快疯了。他只是说，你需要多祈祷。你需要克服它。这就是他说的全部——你需要多祈祷。

00:37:20.930 --> 00:37:33.370
<v Will Ream>And I just thought about that, and I thought, huh. You're turning into what I used-- or you're giving me what I used to give my wife. And there was no life left in me at that point.
<v Will Ream>我反思了那句话，我想，呵。你变得和我以前一样了——或者说你给了我以前给妻子的东西。在那一点上，我心中已经毫无生机了。

00:37:33.370 --> 00:37:50.360
<v Will Ream>I would walk at 2, 3, 4 o'clock in the morning. All through the night, just pace, walk up in the canyons, walk just everywhere, just trying to get some kind of clarity.
<v Will Ream>我会凌晨2点、3点、4点走路。整夜整夜地，踱步，走在峡谷里，走在到处，只是为了试图得到某种清晰的认知。

00:37:50.360 --> 00:38:08.750
<v Stephin  Merritt>(SINGING) I had this big blank sheet of paper, where I was going to write my reasons to live. Or why not end it all today? And kids was the only reason I could give.
<v Stephin  Merritt>（歌唱）我有一张巨大的白纸，我本打算写下我活下去的理由。或者为什么不今天就结束这一切？而孩子是我能给出的唯一理由。

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:24.310
<v Stephin  Merritt>I felt betrayed by my religion, and by the only person I had ever loved, and opened up to. You bet I felt betrayed.
<v Stephin  Merritt>我感到被我的宗教，以及我曾经爱过并向其敞开心扉的唯一一个人背叛了。你敢打赌我感到被背叛了。

00:38:24.310 --> 00:38:42.970
<v Stephin  Merritt>They all said pray. Yeah, well, I prayed. I've had the knife sitting in front of  me. I've had the pills sitting in front of me.
<v Stephin  Merritt>他们都说祈祷。是的，好吧，我祈祷了。刀就放在我面前。药丸就放在我面前。

00:38:42.970 --> 00:38:57.750
<v Stephin  Merritt>And I've been sitting right up on the cliff's edge, with one foot dangling off the ledge. And kids was the only reason I could give.
<v Stephin  Merritt>我一直坐在悬崖边上，一只脚悬在崖边。而孩子是我能给出的唯一理由。

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:09.640
<v Stephin  Merritt>They all said pray. Yeah, well, I prayed.
<v Stephin  Merritt>他们都说祈祷。是的，好吧，我祈祷了。

00:39:09.640 --> 00:39:24.590
<v Miki Meek>When the church told kids they weren't allowed to play, that was the last straw for Will. He'd already lost his wife, and now his whole life was about trying to make things OK for his kids again. He had a radical thought-- maybe they just had to get out and start over.
<v Miki Meek>当教会告诉孩子们不准玩耍时，那是威尔最后的底线。他已经失去了妻子，而现在他整个生命就是试着让孩子们重新过得好起来。他有一个激进的想法——也许他们必须离开并重新开始。

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:35.180
<v Miki Meek>So on his 33rd birthday, Will loaded his kids into a van, and they headed north. Once they got 20 miles away, he stopped the car and explained to his kids what he was doing.
<v Miki Meek>所以在他的33岁生日那天，威尔把孩子们装进一辆货车，他们向北行驶。一旦他们离开20英里远，他停下车，向孩子们解释他正在做的事情。

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:48.410
<v Will Ream>I says, we're going to go and make a life for ourselves somewhere else. I asked them how they felt about that. And they all said that they wanted to come and build a new life.
<v Will Ream>我说，我们要去别的地方为自己创造生活。我问他们对此有什么感觉。他们都说他们想来创造新的生活。

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:51.960
<v Miki Meek>Were you scared at all at that time? Did you doubt yourself at all?
<v Miki Meek>你那时候害怕吗？你怀疑过自己吗？

00:39:51.960 --> 00:40:04.550
<v Will Ream>I wondered. Oh yeah. I really did. I wondered if we were all going to be destroyed tomorrow, and I screwed up. Maybe a week from now.
<v Will Ream>我怀疑过。哦，是的。我真的怀疑过。我怀疑我们明天会不会都被摧毁，我把事情搞砸了。也许一周之后。

00:40:04.550 --> 00:40:33.690
<v Will Ream>But I actually felt like hope. It was genuine hope for the first time in a long time, that I could give them another shot at life. It was like taking a breath of air after you've had your head under water. You know?
<v Will Ream>但我真的感觉到了希望。这是很长一段时间以来第一次有真正的希望，我可以给他们另一个生活的机会。就像在把头潜入水底后，呼吸到了一口空气一样。你知道吗？

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:46.880
<v Miki Meek>Will ended up way north in a city called Ogden, not far from Salt Lake. He and his kids moved into a small apartment, and started living among regular people for the first time. Doing stuff regular people do, like going to the movies.
<v Miki Meek>威尔最终向北到达了一个叫奥格登的城市，离盐湖城不远。他和孩子们搬进了一间小公寓，第一次和普通人生活在一起。做普通人做的事情，比如去电影院。

00:40:46.880 --> 00:41:06.490
<v Will Ream>We went and saw Despicable Me , and it was in 3D. And it was the first movie they'd ever seen in their life. And I was watching them jump, bouncing in their seats and giggling and laughing, and jumping backwards when it looked like stuff was coming out of the screen. And I watched them more than I watched the movie.
<v Will Ream>我们去看了《卑鄙的我》，是3D的。这是他们一生中看过的第一部电影。我看着他们跳起来，在座位上蹦蹦跳跳，咯咯笑，大笑，当看起来东西从屏幕里出来时向后跳。我看着他们的时间比看电影的时间还要多。

00:41:06.490 --> 00:41:16.800
<v Will Ream>And I got to see them be genuinely happy, and not get put in check. And to just be a child. And it was amazing.
<v Will Ream>我能够看到他们真正快乐，没受到压制。能够只是当一个孩子。这真是太棒了。

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:28.490
<v Miki Meek>The four girls all cut their hair. They painted their nails and got their ears pierced. All of that was forbidden back where they used to live. And for the first time in their lives, they played with kids who weren't fundamentalists.
<v Miki Meek>四个女儿都剪了头发。她们涂了指甲，打了耳洞。这一切在他们过去住的地方都是被禁止的。而且这是她们一生中第一次和不是原教旨主义者的孩子玩。

00:41:28.490 --> 00:41:35.050
<v Miki Meek>Back in their old lives, they had names for people like that-- Gentiles, apostates, wicked.
<v Miki Meek>在他们过去的生活中，他们有叫那些人的名字——外邦人（Gentiles），叛教者，邪恶的。

00:41:35.050 --> 00:41:46.430
<v Will Ream>Well, we were at a park in Ogden one time. And they were playing on the swings, and there were some other children there. And one of the other children's mothers came up, and was helping them and stuff.
<v Will Ream>有一次我们在奥格登的一个公园里。她们在秋千上玩，那里还有其他孩子。其中一个孩子的妈妈走过来，帮了她们之类的。

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:59.680
<v Will Ream>And I came walking up, and my oldest daughter was like, dad, you know, the wicked people are pretty nice. And the other lady just glared at me. And I was just like, I'm sorry. I didn't really know what to say.
<v Will Ream>我走过去，大女儿说，爸爸，你知道，邪恶的人人挺好的。另一个女士狠狠瞪了我一眼。我只是说，对不起。我真的不知道该说什么。

00:41:59.680 --> 00:42:01.170
<v Miki Meek>You're like shrugging your shoulders.
<v Miki Meek>你耸了耸肩。

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:06.990
<v Will Ream>Yeah, I didn't really know what to say. And I just says, hon, these people aren't wicked. They're just like us.
<v Will Ream>是啊，我真的不知道该说什么。我只是说，宝贝，这些人不邪恶。他们和我们一样。

00:42:06.990 --> 00:42:20.000
<v Will Ream>And they would ask me all of the time, so are we apostates now? Are we Gentiles now? So they were concerned about being able to go to heaven. Like, you'd hear them talking about it between themselves.
<v Will Ream>他们一直问我，所以我们现在是叛教者吗？我们现在是外邦人吗？所以她们很担心能不能上天堂。就像，你会听到她们自己在谈论这个。

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:36.580
<v Will Ream>They would be like, we went to a movie, and movies are wicked, so we're wicked people. And one of the other ones would say, no, we're not wicked people. That was fun. Dad said it was OK.
<v Will Ream>她们会说，我们看了电影，电影是邪恶的，所以我们是邪恶的人。其中一个会说，不，我们不是邪恶的人。那很有趣。爸爸说是可以的。

00:42:36.580 --> 00:43:07.470
<v Stephin  Merritt>(SINGING) Back in Colorado City, we did not associate with colored people. But in Ogden, it's OK. These two kids who spoke Swahilli-- we all had this barbecue, and my girls loved them. It was just like night and day, seeing my kids play.
<v Stephin  Merritt>（歌唱）回到科罗拉多城，我们不和有色人种交往。但在奥格登，没问题。那两个说斯瓦希里语的孩子——我们举行了烧烤会，我的女儿们很喜欢他们。看到我的孩子们玩耍，简直是天壤之别。

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:34.810
<v Stephin  Merritt>Back in Colorado City, wearing shorts is like rebelling before God. But in Ogden, they don't care, so we wore just what we wanted. Girls went shopping, skirts and dresses, and played music. We could never do that there. Every one would stare.
<v Stephin  Merritt>回到科罗拉多城，穿短裤就像在上帝面前叛逆。但在奥格登，他们不在乎，所以我们穿我们想穿的。女孩子们去购物，买裙子和连衣裙，播放音乐。我们在那里永远做不到那样。每个人都会盯着看。

00:43:34.810 --> 00:43:54.020
<v Stephin  Merritt>We couldn't wear red, because maybe that's what Jesus would wear. We couldn't wear anything bought in a store. We couldn't eat sugar, play games. The kids couldn't have toys, or doing anything fun anymore.
<v Stephin  Merritt>我们不能穿红色，因为也许那是耶稣会穿的颜色。我们不能穿任何商店里买的东西。我们不能吃糖，玩游戏。孩子们不再有玩具，也不能做任何有趣的事了。

00:43:54.020 --> 00:44:12.160
<v Stephin  Merritt>But in Ogden, we had parties. And the older kids went on roller coasters. And they went and got their hair done.
<v Stephin  Merritt>但在奥格登，我们开了派对。大一点的孩子坐了过山车。她们去做头发。

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:23.520
<v Miki Meek>They had a good summer, a fun summer. But when fall came, it was like reality hit. The kids were going to public school for the first time in their lives. They'd been home-schooled before that.
<v Miki Meek>她们过了一个美好的夏天，一个快乐的夏天。但秋天来临时，现实就像冲击了过来。孩子们有生以来第一次上公立学校。在此之前她们是在家上学的。

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:32.590
<v Miki Meek>And Will decided to go to school, too. He only had an eighth grade education, so he got his GED, and then enrolled as a freshman at the local college.
<v Miki Meek>威尔决定也去上学。他只有八年级教育程度，所以他拿到了GED证书，然后作为大一新生进入了当地大学。

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:45.400
<v Miki Meek>It was incredibly tough for Will, trying to hold everything together, the five kids, by himself. When he left the restrictions of their old life, he also left behind all the love and support that came with it.
<v Miki Meek>对威尔来说这非常艰难，试着独自撑起一切，五个孩子。当他离开旧生活的限制时，他也抛弃了随之而来的所有爱与支持。

00:44:45.400 --> 00:45:01.040
<v Miki Meek>For the first time in Will's life, he had no family members, no church members, nobody to come over and watch his kids for a night, or to bring a meal. He'd been living in this tightly knit small town, where everybody looked out for each other, where everybody had known him since he was a kid.
<v Miki Meek>威尔一生中第一次，没有家人，没有教堂成员，没有人能过来帮他看一个晚上的孩子，或者带来一顿饭。他一直生活在这个紧密联系的小镇上，每个人都互相照顾，每个人从他小时候就认识他。

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:09.720
<v Miki Meek>And now, he was far away, and they'd all completely cut him off. One of his sisters called him up one day just to tell him that she'd never talk to him again.
<v Miki Meek>而现在，他远在天边，他们完全切断了与他的联系。他一个姐妹有一天打电话过来只是为了告诉他她永远不会再和他说话了。

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:21.470
<v Miki Meek>Will got increasingly overwhelmed. He couldn't see how things were going to work out. He had no long-term plan for how to support himself and these five children. They were living off his savings, because he was in school.
<v Miki Meek>威尔变得越来越不知所措。他看不出事情会怎样解决。他没有长期计划来支撑自己和这五个孩子。他们靠他的积蓄生活，因为他在上学。

00:45:21.470 --> 00:45:29.960
<v Miki Meek>All his energy had gone into figuring out how to get out. And they got out, but now what? One day in class, Will lost it.
<v Miki Meek>他所有的精力都花在了如何离开上。他们离开了，但现在呢？有一天在课堂上，威尔崩溃了。

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:51.940
<v Will Ream>Just something snapped. I just broke down. I started weeping, and I couldn't stop. It was really hard, and incredibly lonely, when you're 33 years old, to learn how to live again. To basically start from square one.
<v Will Ream>有什么东西断了。我只是崩溃了。我开始哭泣，无法停止。当你33岁时，学习如何重新生活，简直是从零开始，这真的很艰难，也异常孤独。

00:45:51.940 --> 00:45:59.080
<v Miki Meek>Will went home and curled up on the couch for three or four hours, crying. Then he went and picked up his kids from school.
<v Miki Meek>威尔回家蜷缩在沙发上哭了两三个小时。然后他去学校接孩子们。

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:12.100
<v Will Ream>And then, I started making dinner. And then, it was like I was kind of out of it. And when I came back to, I just started looking around. And I asked my oldest daughter, did you check on the food in the oven?
<v Will Ream>然后，我开始做晚饭。然后，我感觉自己有点恍惚。当我回过神来，我开始四处张望。我问大女儿，你检查烤箱里的食物了吗？

00:46:12.100 --> 00:46:28.980
<v Will Ream>And she's like, we already ate, Dad. I looked around, and my oldest daughter had gotten the dinner out of the oven, and served the children and fed them. And they were done, and she'd cleared the dishes off the table, and they were sitting in the seat. And I realized it was three hours later. It freaked me out.
<v Will Ream>她说，我们已经吃过了，爸爸。我环顾四周，大女儿已经把晚餐从烤箱里拿出来，端给孩子们喂饱了。他们吃完了，她把盘子从桌上清理干净，他们坐在位子上。我意识到已经过了三个小时了。我吓坏了。

00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:43.690
<v Miki Meek>Will thinks that for three hours, he was just standing in one spot in the kitchen, as his kids walked around him and served themselves dinner. In those three hours, Will could have set fire to the casserole, or the apartment. A lot could have gone wrong.
<v Miki Meek>威尔认为在那三个小时里，他只是呆呆地站在厨房的一个位置，孩子们在他身边走来走去，自己吃饭。在那三个小时里，威尔本可能点燃了砂锅，或者公寓。很多事情可能出错。

00:46:43.690 --> 00:46:59.790
<v Miki Meek>Will's only goal at this point in his life was to be a good dad to these kids. Now he felt like he was putting them in real danger. His five kids were all young. The oldest was 11, and the youngest was four. I am not stable, he thought.
<v Miki Meek>威尔此时生活中的唯一目标就是做一个好爸爸。现在他觉得自己在让他们处于真正的危险之中。他的五个孩子都很小。最大的11岁，最小的四岁。我想我并不稳定，他想。

00:46:59.790 --> 00:47:09.100
<v Miki Meek>So Will started talking to some counselors. They told him that his situation was serious. He needed to let someone help him care for his kids. So he started looking for help.
<v Miki Meek>所以威尔开始和一些咨询师交谈。他们告诉他情况很严重。他需要让别人帮他照顾孩子。所以他开始寻求帮助。

00:47:09.100 --> 00:47:27.240
<v Miki Meek>And within a week, Will's friends-- families he was just getting to know-- agreed to let his kids live with them, just for a little while, just until Will got back on his feet. Will tried to explain this to his kids-- that he wasn't doing so well, that he needed someone to help with them. And then his 11-year-old piped up.
<v Miki Meek>一周之内，威尔的朋友——他刚开始认识的家庭——同意让他们帮他养孩子，只是暂时的，直到威尔重新振作起来。威尔试图向孩子们解释这件事——他现在状态不好，他需要有人帮他。然后11岁的大女儿插话了。

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:37.330
<v Will Ream>And that's when she says, well, I can, Dad. And it just made me feel worse. And I just told her, I says, I can't allow you to do that. I can't. I says, you need to be a kid.
<v Will Ream>就在那时她说，爸爸，我可以帮忙。那让我感觉更糟糕。我只是告诉她，我说，我不能让你这么做。我不能。我说，你需要当个孩子。

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:51.580
<v Will Ream>They didn't understand. I mean, they're just like, well, a week? Do we get to come home next weekend? And it was hard. Because I couldn't give them a period of time. I didn't know.
<v Will Ream>她们不明白。我的意思是，她们只是觉得，嗯，一周？我们下个周末可以回家吗？这很艰难。因为我无法给出确切的时间。我不知道。

00:47:51.580 --> 00:48:02.910
<v Will Ream>And I was just hoping that somehow, some way, that I would just grow back. That I would be able to, that I'd be able to stand up and take care of it.
<v Will Ream>我只是希望某种程度上，我会好起来。我会能够，我会能够站起来照顾她们。

00:48:02.910 --> 00:48:16.450
<v Miki Meek>That's not what happened, though. What was supposed to be temporary turned into two years. That's when I first talked to Will. He was living in a house with a beat-up couch and a mattress and not a lot more, and doing pretty badly.
<v Miki Meek>但那并没有发生。本应是暂时的却变成了两年。那就是我第一次和威尔谈话的时候。他住在一栋破旧的房子里，有一张破旧的沙发和床垫，几乎没别的东西了，过得相当糟糕。

00:48:16.450 --> 00:48:28.150
<v Miki Meek>He'd dropped out of school and taken a construction job to support the family. But it just wasn't enough. He'd spiraled into a depression where all he could do was work and sleep. This is from that first interview.
<v Miki Meek>他退学了，找了一份建筑工作来养家。但这根本不够。他陷入了抑郁，所有能做的只是工作和睡觉。这是第一次采访的内容。

00:48:28.150 --> 00:48:47.820
<v Will Ream>I'll still have some pretty bad lows, some pretty bad days sometimes, where I need to just kind of disappear into the ether and think about things. And it's really hard. Really hard for me to deal with.
<v Will Ream>我有时仍然会有很严重的低谷，很糟糕的日子，我需要消失在虚空中思考事情。这真的很难。对我来说处理起来真的很难。

00:48:47.820 --> 00:49:03.360
<v Will Ream>I feel like I've failed, kind of. Where I'm not taking care of my kids anymore, my family's not together anymore, stuff like that. It feels like I've had to admit failure, which is hard.
<v Will Ream>我觉得我某种程度上失败了。我不再照顾孩子了，我的家庭不再完整了，之类的。感觉我不得不承认失败，这很艰难。

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:30.470
<v Will Ream>But I always identified myself as their dad. But hopefully, I can get into a place where-- I'd really like to be dad again.
<v Will Ream>但我一直认为自己是她们的爸爸。但希望，我能达到一个地方……我真的想再次成为爸爸。

00:49:30.470 --> 00:49:42.310
<v Miki Meek>His daughters were only 20 minutes away, but it was hard for Will to see them. He'd go over and read books and play games with them. And then when he had to leave, they'd always ask, when do we get to come home?
<v Miki Meek>女儿们住在离他只有20分钟车程的地方，但威尔很难见到她们。他会过去读书，和她们玩游戏。然后当他必须离开时，她们总是会问，我们什么时候回家？

00:49:42.310 --> 00:49:57.820
<v Miki Meek>Will never had an answer for them. His life was still a mess. And so he'd drive away, feeling totally wrecked that he'd let them down. Will started going to see them less often, because it was so painful. And because it seemed like the more he stayed away, the better they did.
<v Miki Meek>威尔从来没有答案。他的生活依然是一团糟。所以他开车离开时，感到彻底崩溃，因为他让她们失望了。威尔开始减少去看她们的次数，因为那太痛苦了。而且因为看起来他离开得越久，她们过得越好。

00:49:57.820 --> 00:50:12.610
<v Miki Meek>Seeing him raised upsetting questions. And when he stayed away, they thrived, settled into their new lives, and made friends. They played soccer, which the girls never would have done, back in their old community in Colorado City. They got to be kids.
<v Miki Meek>见到他引起了令人不安的问题。而当他远离时，她们茁壮成长，在她们的新生活中安定下来，交了朋友。她们踢足球，在科罗拉多城老家时女孩子们是绝不会那样做的。她们能够当孩子了。

00:50:12.610 --> 00:50:29.560
<v Miki Meek>And so Will came to a hard, awful conclusion. He needed to let them move on, without the question of coming home always hanging over their heads. And so after they've been living with their new families for two years, Will waived his parental rights. He let the families adopt his kids.
<v Miki Meek>所以威尔得出了一个艰难、可怕的结论。他需要让她们继续前行，不再有关于回家的问题一直挂在心头。所以当她们在寄养家庭生活了两年后，威尔放弃了他的监护权。他让这些家庭收养了他的孩子。

00:50:29.560 --> 00:50:35.090
<v Miki Meek>Giving up your kids, you gave them a better life. But it didn't necessarily do that exact thing for you.
<v Miki Meek>放弃你的孩子，你给了她们更好的生活。但这并不一定对你本人有同样的效果。

00:50:35.090 --> 00:50:53.350
<v Will Ream>It didn't do that exact thing for me. But that's OK. I'm fine with that. Because they have what I wanted them to have now. They have what I couldn't give them otherwise.
<v Will Ream>这对我本人并没有那个效果。但没关系。我对此没意见。因为她们现在有了我想要她们有的东西。她们有了我否则无法给她们的东西。

00:50:53.350 --> 00:51:09.380
<v Miki Meek>Will had done what he originally wanted to do when he left Colorado City. He'd given his kids a better life. The girls' adopted mom told me that his daughters now see that's what he intended, that he'd tried to do what was best for them, though it took them a while to see it that way.
<v Miki Meek>威尔做到了他在离开科罗拉多城时最初想要做的事情。他给了孩子们更好的生活。女孩们的收养妈妈告诉我，女儿们现在明白了那是他的初衷，他试着做对她们最好的事，虽然她们花了一段时间才那样看。

00:51:09.380 --> 00:51:20.500
<v Miki Meek>It's gotten easier for Will to visit his kids and Skype with them. He used to keep all their photos boxed up. It was too hard to look at them. But now his bedroom shelves are lined with their pictures.
<v Miki Meek>威尔去探望孩子和与她们视频通话变得容易了些。他以前把所有的照片都锁在盒子里。看它们太痛苦了。但现在他卧室的架子上摆满了她们的照片。

00:51:20.500 --> 00:51:38.090
<v Miki Meek>The kids' adopted families live farther away now. That's hard. The son is now in southern Utah, and his four daughters are in another state. And so Will took a job driving oil trucks in Texas and North Dakota. The pay was better, and he no longer had a reason to stick around Salt Lake full time.
<v Miki Meek>孩子们收养的家庭现在住得更远了。这很艰难。儿子现在在犹他州南部，四个女儿在另一个州。所以威尔找了一份在得克萨斯州和北达科他州开油罐车的工作。工资更好，他也没有理由继续全职待在盐湖城了。

00:51:38.090 --> 00:51:47.130
<v Miki Meek>At each point since leaving Colorado City, Will made what seemed like the best choice for his family. But as a result, he doesn't have a family anymore.
<v Miki Meek>自从离开科罗拉多城以来，威尔在每一个点上都做了对家庭看起来最好的选择。但结果就是，他再也没有家庭了。

00:51:47.130 --> 00:52:06.050
<v Will Ream>I miss them. All day, every day. They've never left my mind. I think about the way they used to laugh. Their little oddities, the weird little things they would say. The way they would walk, when they learned to walk. How long their mom was in labor with them, the way they looked when they were born.
<v Will Ream>我想她们。整天，每一天。她们从未离开我的脑海。我想起她们过去笑的样子。她们的小怪癖，她们说的奇怪的小事。她们走路的样子，她们学走路的时候。她们妈妈怀她们的时间有多长，她们出生时的样子。

00:52:06.050 --> 00:52:10.170
<v Miki Meek>If you could somehow go back in time, would you?
<v Miki Meek>如果你能以某种方式回到过去，你会吗？

00:52:10.170 --> 00:52:22.420
<v Will Ream>If I could go back and have everything that I've experienced been removed from my plate, and just dropped straight back into that, into the same mindset-- I might. It was a good life.
<v Will Ream>如果我能回到过去，把我所经历的一切从盘子里移除，直接回到那个状态，到同样的心态——我可能会。那是一段美好的生活。

00:52:22.420 --> 00:52:47.990
<v Will Ream>I refer to that point in my life as having everything, back when I had everything. And that was the pinnacle of my life. That was when everything made sense, and I had everything I ever wanted.
<v Will Ream>我把自己那段生活称为拥有了一切，回到我拥有一切的时候。那是我的生命巅峰。那是所有事情都有意义，我拥有一切我想要东西的时候。

00:52:47.990 --> 00:53:23.560
<v Stephin  Merritt>(SINGING) I've lost hope, and I lost my youth. Lost my church and my hold on truth. I lost my health. I can't do nine to five. But I could still drive.
<v Stephin  Merritt>（歌唱）我失去了希望，失去了青春。失去了我的教堂和对真理的掌握。我失去了健康。我干不了朝九晚五。但我还能开车。

00:53:23.560 --> 00:53:53.840
<v Stephin  Merritt>I started life again at 33. I hauled my ass to Texas to be me. To save my sanity. I lost my family. All the doors slammed shut.
<v Stephin  Merritt>我33岁时重新开始了生活。我把我的屁股带到得克萨斯做自己。为了保住我的理智。我失去了家人。所有门都关上了。

00:53:53.840 --> 00:54:41.060
<v Stephin  Merritt>I know who I am, but I'm not sure what. I did what I had to do to survive. I don't sleep too well, but I can still drive.
<v Stephin  Merritt>我知道我是谁，但我不知道是什么。我做了我必须做的来生存。我睡得不太好，但我还能开车。

00:54:41.060 --> 00:54:52.770
<v Ira Glass>Original songs and scoring by Stephin Merritt, with Sam Davol on cello and glockenspiel. Pinky Weitzman on viola. Engineering by Charles Newman. Miki Meek reported this story. Miki is one of the producers of our show.
